[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114828,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Peripheral giant cell granuloma (PGCG) is a reactive localized proliferation of mononuclear cells and osteoclasts in a vascular stroma located in the soft tissues of the gingiva or alveolar ridge mucosa.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114829,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114830,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA0D.Y Other specified disorders of gingival or edentulous alveolar ridge</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114831,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> giant cell epulis.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> peripheral reparative giant cell granuloma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114832,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114833,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PGCG arises in the gingiva or alveolar ridge, more commonly in the mandible than the maxilla {29569293; 25240995; 17428697}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114834,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A sessile or pedunculated lump with a smooth, ulcerated, or nodular surface and often a characteristic red, maroon, or purple colour. PGCG may reach several centimetres in diameter, and erosion of the underlying bone is found in almost one&nbsp;third of all cases {17428697; 25240995}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>Radiographic assessment should exclude a central giant cell granuloma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114835,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "<p >The epidemiology is unknown. PGCG is the commonest oral giant cell lesion. It is slightly more prevalent in women than in men (M:F&nbsp;ratio: 1:1.2) and arises over a wide age distribution.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T17:36:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114836,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PGCG is caused by local irritation of the mucoperiosteum or the coronal part of the periodontal ligament by dental calculus, plaque deposits, retained tooth roots, or other chronic irritation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114837,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Similarly to central giant cell granulomas, about 70% of PGCGs harbour <em>KRAS</em> mutations, mainly in codons&nbsp;146 and&nbsp;12, and 10% have <em>FGFR1</em> p.C381R or <em>FGFR1</em> p.N330I mutations {30385747}. Notably, when associated with dental implants, PGCG also harbours <em>KRAS</em> mutations {31758745}. MAPK pathway activation is observed in PGCG irrespective of the presence of mutations {30385747}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114838,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PGCG appears as fleshy red-brown tissue due to vascularity and haemorrhage. When formalin-fixed, it has a solid consistency with a dark-brown coloration.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114839,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PGCG resembles central giant cell granuloma, with an unencapsulated proliferation of mononuclear spindle-shaped and polygonal cells with osteoclast multinucleated cells in a vascular loose fibrous tissue. Foci of haemorrhage, haemosiderin pigment, and scattered deposits of immature bone are frequent. An inflammatory component of fibrous or pyogenic granuloma&ndash;like hyperplasia may be present, reflecting local irritant causes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114840,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114841,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 588,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 761,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 591,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114842,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 591,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> location on the gingiva or alveolar ridge; cellular mononuclear stroma; clustered osteoclasts; haemorrhage; no origin from underlying intraosseous giant cell granuloma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> exclusion of hyperparathyroidism (very rarely associated with localized lesions).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 759,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 589,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114843,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 589,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 760,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 590,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114844,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 590,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The rate of recurrence after conservative surgical removal may be 16%. Curettage to periodontal ligament or peripheral osteotomy reduce recurrence, especially when the underlying bone is eroded {29569293}. Any source of irritation should be removed.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T11:51:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]